\f1\fs22 Inside, Lonnie felt the quiet on her skin the way a mother coming home from a shift in the laundry mines could tell her children were safely in bed the moment she walked through the front door. The vacant hallway was warmer than empty. Lonnie stopped and listened, thought she heard whispers, and something like humming.\
The kitchen was empty. So was the office. No one in the staff room. She didn\'92t check the toilets on the way to the showroom\'85 no one behind the bar either. She could hear someone speaking in a strange monotone, some words\'85\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 You will seek me and find me when you seek me with all your heart.
\f1\i0 \
\'85then a mumbled response from a chorus of voices.\
Lonnie took two more wary steps. Another voice, female and clearer\'85\

\f2\i He has sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim freedom for the captives and release from darkness for the prisoners.
\f1\i0 \
Lonnie froze. Prisoners? Captives?\
The next thing she thought she heard was, 
\f2\i Prepare the way; make straight in the desert a highway for your Cadillacs.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i That sounds more Elvi
\f1\i0 , she thought.\
The closer she got to the showroom, the clearer the words:\

\f2\i I will sing for the one I love a song about his vineyard: My loved one had a vineyard on a fertile hillside.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Lonnie was moving again\'97into the showroom, around the end of the bar. The float lay half-counted along the bar, coins abandoned mid-tower. A mop leaned in its bucket in still, grey water. Past the booths, through the chairs still stacked on tables, Lonnie could see much of the prep crew gathered in loose concentric circles.\
They were kneeling.\
And swaying, all of them, slightly out of time with each other, yet somehow more together for it, their cheeks tear-streaked, their eyes bright with rapture as if they\'92d been lost for decades and had finally made it home.\
Lonnie was certain she was as far from home as she\'92d ever been.\
Susan, a Speedway series and the bar\'92s accountant, straightened, threw her hands to the heavens. \'93How great Thou art!\'94\
Lonnie\'92s heart chilled before she had time to decode the words.\
Deke, who worked the bar when there weren\'92t enough bodies dumped in the harbour to support his main gig as a diver, stood and peeled away from the group, as smooth as he was on roller skates. He went to the jukebox, laid a hand on its glass the way one might lay a hand on a coffin or a horse, and very tenderly pressed buttons on the console to make a selection.\
Lonnie was already across the floor as the jukebox clicked and considered. Its arm woke and reached for a disc. Lonnie seized its power cable in both hands and tore the plug out of the wall.\
\'93Hey, I was playing that,\'94 Deke complained. Lonnie shoved him aside, went at the front of the machine, and found the catch without looking. The front swung wide, exposing the carousel.\
Lonnie pulled a record and examined it with suspicion, turned it over a few times to make sure. Both surfaces were dull and the labels faded; the disc felt genuine, familiar. Lonnie grunted, rotated the carousel and pulled another record. It felt right in her hands too. She set it down on top of the first on the floor. Then another.\
\'93Hey, what are you doing?\'94 Deke asked, more curious than confrontational.\
\'93Maintenance,\'94 Lonnie answered as she pulled another disc.\
Deke blinked and turned back for the prayer group on stage.\
Lonnie wondered if she was just crazy. She turned the carousel while she rethought her strategy. A flash of light glinted from the edge of one of the discs. Lonnie turned the carousel back and then forward again. A disc glinted again like it was made of satin. Lonnie pulled it and caught her breath. The record was glossy and fresh, the label bright. She read the titles on both sides.\
\'93Taxing gospel music?\'94 Like every Elvi, Lonnie knew gospel music voided their warranties, though none of them knew why or what the consequences might be. She doubted anyone in the country even knew what it was, other than it was popular in older and unfashionable times, the same as other genres like Acid Bro Country, Disco Polo or Mormon Dubstep.\
Then the room began to sing. Where they knelt, cooks and cleaners, maintenance and management sang what would be known as 
\f2\i Crying in the Chapel 
\f1\i0 in less decrepit universes, even in those that had endured 
\f2\i Having Fun with Elvis On Stage 
\f1\i0 (\'93a talking album only\'94).\
Lonnie felt magnetised; she was instantly tugged to join her co-workers. She longed to join her co-workers, to join the singing of a song she might have been built to sing. Her rib cage felt like the bars of a jail cell; she knew she\'92d have to resist, or The Memphis and everyone who worked there would never be the same again. She clenched her jaw, shut her eyes, and clenched every muscle in her legs against walking forward.\
Still, the tune compelled her with unnatural power.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 No, not the tune
\f1\i0 .\
The 
\f2\i harmony
\f1\i0 .\
The crew were singing in rapturous, perfect four-part harmony, and that frightened Lonnie more than anything else. Everyone knew that no Elvis could sing harmony for shit; plenty had been fired from non-Elvi bands for it. And she would have been even more frightened if she could only know \'85 but how could she? The words her crew were singing were the multiversally normal ones. In singing gospel, there was no squabbling over lyrics.\
Charlie, a Roustabout series, noticed Lonnie standing stiff, with her eyes forced shut and her hands over her ears. He waved the crew down and, one by one, they stopped singing. He stood and faced Lonnie, who had relaxed once the singing had completely stopped.\
\'93Hey, Lonnie,\'94 Charlie said, trying to soothe her. \'93Why don\'92t you come join us.\'94\
Lonnie vigorously shook her head, denying her own urges as much as Charlie.\
Charlie smiled. \'93Okay, we understand.\'94\
Lonnie knew that smile, as any Elvi would, the smile that made them irresistible, and had probably saved them from extinction in the days before emancipation. Lonnie was glad for it, in the same moment that she took a step back.\
The rest of the crew stood. They seemed anything but threatening, but Lonnie took another step back anyway.\
\'93Hey,\'94 Charlie said as if having a new thought. \'93How about we play some songs? There\'92s some good new ones in the Wurlitzer.\'94\
\'93It\'92s gospel music,\'94 Lonnie said flat.\
\'93It\'92s wonderful.\'94 Charlie opened his arms and showed his palms.\
\'93It\'92s against the warranty.\'94\
\'93The warranty oppresses us, Lonnie. We\'92re free now.\'94\
\'93We\'92ve been free since emancipation,\'94 Lonnie countered.\
\'93Then we are free to listen to any music we choose.\'94 Charlie shrugged, 
\f2\i Wouldn\'92t you agree?
\f1\i0 \
\'93How great Thou art!\'94 Susan hailed again.\
Charlie waved her down. \'93Let\'92s just listen to a few tunes, Lonnie. Then, I swear, you will understand.\'94\
Lonnie gulped. She wanted to run in two directions at once. \'93Charlie,\'94 she said. She put the warmth in it, the good warmth, the one she used on the big sad units at last call that said, 
\f2\i come on now, mate, let\'92s get you sorted,
\f1\i0  the voice that had brought countless drunks off almost as many ledges.\
Charlie looked at her with such kindness, she almost wanted to put the mint-condition records back in the carousel.\
\'93Truth,\'94 Charlie said. \'93Harmony. A way home.\'94\

\f2\i Harmony
