Charlie came and loomed over her. \'93Put the records back, Lonnie.\'94\
Lonnie glanced up. \'93How did these records get in there, Charlie? You thought about that yet?\'94\
\'93God maybe?\'94\
\'93So God is the jukebox maintenance guy now?\'94 Lonnie challenged.\
\'93Lonnie, don\'92t be like that. It\'92s all okay.\'94\
\'93Is he on the payroll now too? Or just a contractor? Hey, Susan!\'94 Lonnie was shouting by this point. \'93How much are we paying God to keep the Wurlitzer running?\'94\
\'93Don\'92t be stupid, Lonnie. He probably sent an angel.\'94\
\'93An angel? What about a couple of those fat little cherubim of his? Floating down, with a stack of records wedged in the cracks of their plump little butt cheeks? Maybe, that\'92s how it happened. They just floated down through the roof with a buttload of vinyl and blessed us with their taste in music.\'94\
\'93It\'92s a miracle,\'94 Charlie answered.\
\'93It\'92s a heist, you moron! Sabotage! Someone wants to break our warranty!\'94\
\'93You\'92re sounding crazy, Lonnie.\'94\
\'93I\'92m sounding crazy? You\'92re the one claiming a miracle, cherubim gifting us the very music that invalidates our warranty. Aren\'92t you in the slightest bit suspicious?\'94\
